# Python Port Plan — Step 09 · Global Executable

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/09_global_executable` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/09_global_executable` that is behaviourally
equivalent: a real, installable command that works from any directory on
the machine, resolving *which* step's source to actually run in this
priority order — (1) a `BOUKENSHA_PATH` env var pointing at an arbitrary
step folder, (2) a `~/.boukensharc` file containing a single path, (3) the
step bundled inside the installed package itself (this step's own `src/
boukensha/`, i.e. "the latest release") — then boots the interactive REPL
against whichever was resolved.

**Command name: `boukensha-py`, not `boukensha`.** The Ruby gem already
installs a real `boukensha` executable on this machine (confirmed:
`gem install` puts it at `~/.local/share/gem/ruby/3.3.0/bin/boukensha`).
Both implementations are meant to coexist for side-by-side comparison, not
replace one another, so the Python console script is named `boukensha-py`
deliberately — this avoids depending on `$PATH` ordering to decide which
`boukensha` wins (fragile and installation-order-dependent) and lets both
be run deterministically in the same shell. Every `boukensha` reference in
this plan (READMEs, `[project.scripts]`, error messages, the `debug`
loading-from line) means `boukensha-py` for the Python port — noted once
here rather than repeated at every occurrence.

This is the tenth entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>` series (see
[`context.md`](context.md) for the index). `08_the_repl_loop` is the direct
predecessor (not yet ported at the time of this writing — see
[`08_the_repl_loop`](08_the_repl_loop)); this step doesn't add a new
in-process primitive so much as it changes the *distribution model* — from
"a folder you `bundle exec` inside" to "a command on your `$PATH`."

**Note on this step's real, working Ruby state (important for scoping the
port):** `09_global_executable` has **no `examples/` directory at all**
(removed relative to `08_the_repl_loop`) and **no `bin/ruby/
09_global_executable` launcher script exists anywhere in this repo** —
confirmed by checking `week1_baseline/bin/ruby/`, which jumps from `08_
the_repl_loop` straight to `10_standard_tool_library`. This is not an
oversight to fix; it's the whole point of the step. There is nothing to
`ruby examples/whatever.rb` here — the deliverable *is* the installed
`boukensha` command, exercised by actually installing it and running it
from an arbitrary directory. The Python port should follow suit: **no
`examples/example.py` and no `week1_baseline/bin/python/
09_global_executable` launcher for this step** — a real divergence from
every prior step's target layout, not an inconsistency to smooth over.

